{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher, written by the screenplay by scott rudin, jesse oscarson, and aaron sorkin. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben Mezrich.\n\nthe film revolves around the founding of the social networking site facebook, by harvard university students mark zuckerberg, along with his college roommates edward saverin, andrew mccollum, dustin moskovitz, and christopher hughes. the film also focuses on the role of sean parker, a facebook user who becomes a key figure in the site's development.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, and his portrayal is widely regarded as one of the best performances of the year. eisenberg's performance is a masterclass in subtlety, capturing the nuances of zuckerberg's character with incredible accuracy. he brings a depth and complexity to the role, making zuckerberg a relatable and human character.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally impressive, with andrew garfield delivering a standout performance as edward saverin. garfield's performance is a nuanced exploration of saverin's character, capturing his insecurities and vulnerabilities with ease. joshua jackson, jesse wright, and armie hammer also deliver strong performances as the other harvard students involved in the founding of facebook.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its direction by david fincher. fincher brings his signature style to the film, using a muted color palette and a focus on the emotional toll of the events unfolding on screen. the film's cinematography is stunning, capturing the beauty and intensity of the harvard campus.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is also a major highlight of the film. sorkin's writing is sharp and witty, capturing the complexities of the characters and the events unfolding on screen. the dialogue is natural and authentic, making the characters feel real and relatable.\n\nthe film's themes of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success are timely and thought-provoking. the film raises important questions about the impact of social media on our lives and the consequences of our actions.\n\noverall, the social network is a masterpiece of filmmaking. it's a gripping and thought-provoking film that will keep you on the edge of your seat. the performances, direction, and screenplay are all top-notch, making this a must-see film for anyone interested in the world of social media and the people who shape it.\n\nrating: 5/5 stars\n\nnote: this review is based on the theatrical version of the film, not the extended cut.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.9086643e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.933899e-06}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.3342006}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.0031711787}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00043990472}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.011117537}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.00057299406}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.18416922}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0021900972}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.009290322}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00041380903}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -2.682173e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.131186}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.26963526}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -3.5166122e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.8388188}, {"token": " screenplay", "logprob": -0.7850745}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.2083719}, {"token": " sc", "logprob": -3.5000386}, {"token": "ott", "logprob": -0.00023338455}, {"token": " rud", "logprob": -0.83087486}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -5.245195e-06}